A||N||3||KRMFiero||Painting Non-Fiero Cars (Medal)||||1||
Z||000000||KRMFiero||09-24-2001||07:39 PM||||My aunt gave me a '87? buick Skylark that has the Crappy GM paint job that is just pealing right off.... anyway What is the best way to paint this car? I want it like a darker blue.  <BR>Can I use orbit sanders on this car?<BR>what grits?<BR>What should I ask for when i get my base/primer/clear coats/how much?<P>Anyone have any color suggestions?<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000001||GT Bastard||09-24-2001||08:57 PM||||Just a friendly reminder... You want to use "metal," not "medal."  Also, who paints skylarks?  Just drive the hell outta it and let it rust.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||09-25-2001||12:28 PM||||save a lot of time and sandpaper, just get a few new single edge razor blades.Easier too if you get one of those handles. start somewhere its peeling and start shaving holding the blade almost flat on the car. itll come off in long strips of paint to thow in the trash can. It will stick at edges of panels so you will have to sand that off. usually only the upper surfaces like hood, trunk and roof will do it. when you get off all that will shave off, sand with 360 grit all over and paint it. Ive striped the whole top half in a couple of hours.<P>another way that sometimes works, is to take about $10 worth of quarters to power car wash, put wand rite up to the paint and spray the old paint off in long strokes. I stripped a whole Ford Taurus from bumper to bumper to the bare car in 30 minutes.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 09-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fformula88||09-25-2001||12:42 PM||||I think in your situation it might be best to just take it somewhere to get it painted.  I like the razor idea too.  However, by the time you get done paying for sandpaper, paint and all that stuff you could have gotten a Maaco job or something (of course, they aren't that good either).<P>If you do it yourself, be sure to do a lot of wet sanding along the edges of the paint where it has peeled/or been removed.  If there is even the slightes bump between paint and metal, or different coats of paint, it will show thru the finished coat.<P>You can use power sanders too, but be ready to do a lot of hand sanding anyway round edges and to clean it up good with fine paper (like 360 or so).  The danger is creating a flat spot if you don't take ll the paint off with a power sander... I'd recommend some good ole elbow grease especially if you haven't done much body work!  Good luck!<BR><P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero2m6siga.JPG">       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <P>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
